WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.001 --> 00:00:07.844
What hotel must be rebuilt every year? What church can you eat? What kind of house is made of soda bottles?

2
00:00:07.845 --> 00:00:14.680
The Ice Hotel in Sweden must be rebuilt every year because it meltsㅡit's made entirely from ice.

3
00:00:14.681 --> 00:00:23.188
The church in the Wieliczka salt mine in Poland can be eaten, although it is not recommended, because it is carved entirely from salt.

4
00:00:23.190 --> 00:00:28.493
In Spain, Brazil, and the United States, people have made houses from bottles.

5
00:00:28.493 --> 00:00:36.767
When someone drinks a soda and recycles the bottle, that bottle may become a part of an environmentally friendly building, the bottle house.

6
00:00:36.768 --> 00:00:42.416
These are just a few examples of the unique buildings that we can find around the world.

7
00:00:42.417 --> 00:00:44.417
The Ice Hotel of Sweden

8
00:00:44.418 --> 00:00:52.676
Some people prefer to vacation in the sun, but the Ice Hotel has people checking in and chilling out at sub-zero temperatures.

9
00:00:52.677 --> 00:01:01.161
The Ice Hotel has over sixty rooms, a bar, a cinema, and a church. It is built from thousands of tons of snow and ice.

10
00:01:01.162 --> 00:01:09.912
The Ice Hotel lies two hundred kilometers north of the Arctic Circle, near the town of Jukkasjarvi, on the banks of the Torne River in Sweden.

11
00:01:09.913 --> 00:01:17.734
Before the first Ice Hotel was built, very few tourists came to Jukkasjarvi during the long, cold, and snowy winters.

12
00:01:17.735 --> 00:01:21.883
For two weeks of the year in December, there is total darkness.

13
00:01:21.884 --> 00:01:28.376
In 1990, a local adventure-tourism company decided to treat the town's cold winter as an asset.

14
00:01:28.377 --> 00:01:34.369
The company built a large igloo in the shape of a cylinder and held art exhibitions there.

15
00:01:34.370 --> 00:01:39.824
The "igloo gallery" was successful and sparked the idea for an ice hotel.

16
00:01:39.824 --> 00:01:44.153
Some guests visiting the gallery fell asleep there by mistake.

17
00:01:44.154 --> 00:01:52.458
They had reindeer skins to keep them warm, and when they awoke, they felt refreshed, well rested, and, most surprisingly, warm!

18
00:01:52.459 --> 00:01:59.256
Inspired by these guests' experiences, the tourism company built the first Ice Hotel in 1992.

19
00:01:59.257 --> 00:02:06.687
Since then, it has been rebuilt annually. Around thirty local artists and builders start working on the structure each October.

20
00:02:06.688 --> 00:02:12.102
They carve blocks of ice that will make up the foundation, walls, and roof of the hotel.

21
00:02:12.103 --> 00:02:19.181
The ice is crystal clear because it all comes from the Torne River, a mountain river with clean spring water.

22
00:02:19.182 --> 00:02:25.745
By the beginning of December, the main building is almost finished, and work on the interior begins.

23
00:02:25.746 --> 00:02:32.699
Sculptors cut and shape the ice to create windows, beds, tables, chairs, lamps, and decorative sculptures.

24
00:02:32.700 --> 00:02:40.051
The first guests arrive in mid-December, although the sculptors' work inside continues until January.

25
00:02:40.052 --> 00:02:44.529
When you first check into the Ice Hotel, you must change your clothes.

26
00:02:44.530 --> 00:02:50.928
Guests are supplied with a specially made sleeping bag, warm clothes, thermal underwear, and gloves.

27
00:02:50.929 --> 00:03:00.437
You sure need themㅡthe average temperature inside is -5℃. That's a lot warmer than the -40℃ outside!

28
00:03:00.438 --> 00:03:08.407
After seeing a movie in the ice cinema and having a drink in an ice glass at the ice bar, you can go to sleep on your ice bed.

29
00:03:08.408 --> 00:03:10.088
How do you sleep on ice?

30
00:03:10.088 --> 00:03:19.354
Well, your bed, which is mostly a block of ice, also consists of a wooden plank, a foam mattress, sheets, and reindeer skins.

31
00:03:19.355 --> 00:03:25.316
Only the toilets of the hotel, housed in a separate building, are not made of ice. Phew!

32
00:03:25.317 --> 00:03:29.630
In April, the sun's rays start to melt the Ice Hotel.

33
00:03:29.631 --> 00:03:35.740
The last guests check out, and the Ice Hotel slowly but surely becomes a "water hotel."

34
00:03:35.741 --> 00:03:40.639
All the water flows back into the Torne River, from where it came.

35
00:03:40.640 --> 00:03:42.367
The Wieliczka Salt Mine

36
00:03:42.368 --> 00:03:51.430
There are other buildings that look similar to the Ice Hotel, but thankfully, much warmerㅡthe salt buildings inside the Wieliczka salt mine.

37
00:03:51.431 --> 00:03:56.799
The Wieliczka salt mine near Krakow, Poland, is a vast underground city.

38
00:03:56.800 --> 00:04:03.143
It is over seven hundred years old and consists of nine levels with over two thousand chambers.

39
00:04:03.144 --> 00:04:05.527
How did this hidden city come to be?

40
00:04:05.528 --> 00:04:12.903
According to a thirteenth-century legend, Princess Kinga of Hungary was to be married to King Boleslaus of Poland.

41
00:04:12.904 --> 00:04:17.614
Kinga asked her father for a precious gift to give to her fiance.

42
00:04:17.615 --> 00:04:22.678
The people in Poland had everythingㅡwhat could she give that was special and unique?

43
00:04:22.679 --> 00:04:29.905
One thing that Polish people could not get easily was salt; Poland had to import salt from its neighbors.

44
00:04:29.906 --> 00:04:33.609
Back then, salt was indispensable for survival.

45
00:04:33.610 --> 00:04:41.626
It was used for the preservation of meat, butter, and fish; in leather production; and later, for the production of gunpowder.

46
00:04:41.627 --> 00:04:46.494
So, Kinga asked her father for a Hungarian salt mine, and he agreed.

47
00:04:46.495 --> 00:04:55.666
When Princess Kinga visited the salt mine her father had given her, she did a strange thingㅡshe threw her engagement ring into the tunnel!

48
00:04:55.667 --> 00:05:00.409
The princess' servants were shocked, but Princess Kinga was calm.

49
00:05:00.410 --> 00:05:04.738
She left the mine and got ready to meet her husband-to-be in Poland.

50
00:05:04.739 --> 00:05:12.536
After Kinga married King Boleslaus, the princess traveled to Wieliczka, a small town near Krakow in Poland.

51
00:05:12.537 --> 00:05:19.772
She instructed the townspeople to dig a deep hole. They did, and were overjoyed to discover salt.

52
00:05:19.773 --> 00:05:25.187
Finding salt back then was the equivalent of finding oil, or even gold, now.

53
00:05:25.188 --> 00:05:33.165
When the diggers looked at the first block of salt carefully, they discovered something elseㅡKinga's engagement ring was inside.

54
00:05:33.165 --> 00:05:34.805
It was a miracle.

55
00:05:34.806 --> 00:05:45.111
After her death, Princess Kinga was declared the patron saint of salt miners, and the Wieliczka salt mine became the largest salt mine in Europe.

56
00:05:45.112 --> 00:05:51.159
By the fourteenth century, the salt from Wieliczka was generating one-third of Poland's income.

57
00:05:51.160 --> 00:05:54.604
But how did a salt mine become a salt city?

58
00:05:54.605 --> 00:06:02.667
Mining can be a hard and lonely jobㅡimagine spending hours underground, slowly digging into the salt, bit by bit.

59
00:06:02.668 --> 00:06:08.879
One day, a miner decided not to dig, but to chip away at the walls, and let a shape come out.

60
00:06:08.880 --> 00:06:16.404
The result was a small but beautiful sculpture. Slowly, other miners started to carve the salt, too.

61
00:06:16.405 --> 00:06:21.686
They made sculptures and engravings in the salt, and the mine managers encouraged them.

62
00:06:21.687 --> 00:06:31.929
Years passed, and the miners' projects became more ambitiousㅡlike the Chapel of the Blessed Kinga, which is the same size as a regular church.

63
00:06:31.930 --> 00:06:35.149
It is 50 meters long and 12 meters high.

64
00:06:35.150 --> 00:06:48.759
Two things make it unusual: first, it is one hundred meters underground, and second, the altar, pews, religious statues, and even the chandeliers that hang from the ceiling are all made of salt.

65
00:06:48.760 --> 00:06:55.862
Mass is still celebrated here three times a year: on Christmas Day, Miner's Day, and Blessed Kinga Day.

66
00:06:55.863 --> 00:07:09.730
The salt miners did not stop with an underground church; you can also eat lunch in the mine's restaurant, see a movie in the mine's cinema, cross a bridge over an underground lake, and even send a postcard from the mine's post office!

67
00:07:09.731 --> 00:07:19.669
The Wieliczka salt mine is now a UNESCO World Heritage site, which means the artwork and creativity of the salt miners will be preserved forever.

68
00:07:19.670 --> 00:07:21.404
Bottle Houses

69
00:07:21.405 --> 00:07:30.811
There is another unique kind of building that uses a material that is cheap, sturdy, and easy to find. What is it? The humble bottle.

70
00:07:30.812 --> 00:07:37.422
The first bottle house was built in 1902 in Nevada, the United States, during a gold-mining boom.

71
00:07:37.423 --> 00:07:42.407
Gold miners lived in desert camps, and building materials were scarce.

72
00:07:42.408 --> 00:07:50.119
Freight trains often carried supplies of liquor to these camps before bringing other materials, such as wood, cement, and tools.

73
00:07:50.120 --> 00:07:56.862
Saloons were among the first commercial buildings in the mining camps, so there was no shortage of bottles!

74
00:07:56.863 --> 00:08:03.019
Before long, garbage dumps filled with beer, wine, and liquor bottles surrounded these boomtowns.

75
00:08:03.020 --> 00:08:13.903
Some of the gold miners got sick of living in tents and, with no other building materials available, decided to use a material that was plentifulㅡthe empty bottles.

76
00:08:13.904 --> 00:08:19.303
Miners set the bottles in adobe or concrete and built themselves homes.

77
00:08:19.304 --> 00:08:25.218
One house in Tonopah, Nevada, was made from over ten thousand bottles.

78
00:08:25.219 --> 00:08:29.570
For the miners, the best thing about bottles was their costㅡnothing.

79
00:08:29.571 --> 00:08:35.899
As long as the miners kept going to the local saloons, they could keep building their bottle houses.

80
00:08:35.900 --> 00:08:44.400
Nowadays, people build bottle houses for environmental reasonsㅡwhy use new materials when you can reuse and recycle?

81
00:08:44.401 --> 00:08:54.409
In September 2003, Brazilian bar owner Celio de Oliveira began to collect discarded plastic soda bottles from a beach in Rio de Janeiro.

82
00:08:54.410 --> 00:08:58.504
"My main goal was to clean the beach," he later recalled.

83
00:08:58.505 --> 00:09:06.185
"Then I had the idea to build a house using them, and all of a sudden, I had 10,000 bottles."

84
00:09:06.186 --> 00:09:13.201
The result was a home built from nothing but bottles; even its light switches are made from bottle tops.

85
00:09:13.202 --> 00:09:23.171
In Ibiza, Spain, a local environmental group called Greenheart made an eco-office in a bottle house, with computers powered by the sun and the wind.

86
00:09:23.172 --> 00:09:28.305
This bottle house is completely made from recycled and natural materials.

87
00:09:28.306 --> 00:09:34.681
The bottles are joined together with the red soil of Ibiza and strengthened with straw to make walls.

88
00:09:34.682 --> 00:09:39.557
The roof is made from a satellite dish and the top of an old bus shelter.

89
00:09:39.558 --> 00:09:47.026
The windows came from many placesㅡone window is from a car, and there is even a window from a washing machine.

90
00:09:47.028 --> 00:09:54.668
By using a recycled material like bottles, people can build homes that are both unique and kind to the environment.

91
00:09:54.669 --> 00:10:05.700
Whether they are built from snow, salt, or recycled bottles, these amazing buildings show how resourceful and inventive people can create shelter from the things around them.

